## 14. Success Metrics

The pilot should measure whether the AI agent produces meaningful analyst time savings without reducing governance quality.

### Definition of done

The project is considered successful if:

1. A pilot group of analysts uses the system for one week.
2. The pilot group reports that the system saves at least **a couple of hours per analyst per week**.
3. Analysts confirm that outputs are useful, traceable, and grounded in source documents.
4. Draft due diligence reports are sufficiently complete for analyst review and editing.
5. The system identifies missing information and follow-up questions reliably.
6. No material unsupported claims or ungrounded recommendations are observed during pilot testing.
7. Product managers and governance leads agree that the workflow is suitable for broader internal rollout.

If these criteria are met, North should consider rolling the system out more broadly across the firm.

## 15. Pilot Rollout Plan

### Phase 1: Internal prototype

- Use a limited set of historical or synthetic managed portfolio and managed fund submissions.
- Build document ingestion, retrieval, structured extraction, and draft report generation.
- Validate output quality with analysts.

### Phase 2: Analyst pilot

- Invite 2–6 analysts and selected product managers.
- Run the agent on live or controlled product review cases.
- Track time saved, quality issues, missing evidence, and analyst edits.

### Phase 3: Governance review

- Review audit logs, source grounding, answer quality, hallucination risk, and process fit.
- Refine the governance framework, report template, and missing-information checklist.

### Phase 4: Broader rollout decision

- If analysts save at least a couple of hours per week and governance quality is maintained, expand usage to a broader internal user group.

## 16. Risks and Mitigations

| Risk | Description | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Hallucinated analysis | The model may generate confident but unsupported statements. | Require retrieval-grounded responses and citation checks. |
| Inconsistent document quality | Investment manager submissions may vary significantly. | Use structured extraction templates and missing-information flags. |
| Over-reliance by analysts | Users may treat AI drafts as final assessments. | Keep human approval mandatory and label outputs as drafts. |
| Sensitive data exposure | Submissions may include confidential commercial information. | Restrict access, enforce internal hosting, and log usage. |
| Weak adoption | Analysts may not trust the system if outputs are vague or hard to verify. | Prioritise source citations, transparent reasoning, and editable report outputs. |
| Scope creep | Stakeholders may ask for recommendations, external data, or adviser-facing use. | Maintain strict scope boundaries for the pilot. |
| Poor evaluation | Time savings alone may hide quality issues. | Measure accuracy, grounding, missing-information detection, and analyst edit rate. |

## 17. Recommended MVP

The MVP should focus on a narrow but high-value workflow:

> Upload a managed portfolio or managed fund submission package → ask analyst due diligence questions → retrieve evidence → extract structured facts → identify missing information → draft a due diligence report for analyst review.

The MVP should not attempt to automate final approval. It should make analysts faster, more consistent, and better supported.

## 18. Strategic Value

The Investment Governance AI Agent can help North strengthen its platform proposition in three ways.

First, it can reduce product onboarding cycle time by accelerating document review and due diligence drafting. Faster onboarding means advisers can access new managed portfolios and managed funds sooner.

Second, it can improve governance consistency by applying a repeatable framework across products, managers, and documents.

Third, it can free analysts from repetitive extraction and summarisation work, allowing them to spend more time on judgement, risk assessment, stakeholder engagement, and product quality.

For North, this is not just an internal productivity tool. It is a way to improve the operating model behind adviser choice, platform trust, and investment governance at scale.

